.Dd November 2, 2026
.Dt MAC_NTPD 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mac_ntpd
.Nd "policy allowing ntpd to run as non-root user"
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To compile the ntpd policy into your kernel, place the following lines
in your kernel configuration file:
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Cd "options MAC"
.Cd "options MAC_NTPD"
.Ed
.Pp
Alternately, to load the ntpd policy module at boot time,
place the following line in your kernel configuration file:
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Cd "options MAC"
.Ed
.Pp
and in
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mac_ntpd_load="YES"
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
policy grants any process running as user
.Sq ntpd
(uid 123) the privileges needed to manipulate
system time, and to (re-)bind to the privileged NTP port.
.Pp
When
.Xr ntpd 8
is started with
.Sq Fl u Ar <user>[:group]
on the command line, it performs all initializations requiring root
privileges, then drops root privileges by switching to the given user id.
From that point on, the only privileges it requires are the ability
to manipulate system time, and the ability to re-bind a UDP socket
to the NTP port (port 123) after a network interface change.
.Pp
With the
.Nm
policy active, it may also be possible to start ntpd as a non-root user,
because the default ntpd options don't require any additional root
privileges beyond those granted by the policy.
.Ss Privileges Granted
The exact set of kernel privileges granted to any process running
with the configured uid is:
.Bl -inset -compact -offset indent
.It Dv PRIV_ADJTIME
.It Dv PRIV_CLOCK_SETTIME
.It Dv PRIV_NTP_ADJTIME
.It Dv PRIV_NETINET_RESERVEDPORT
.It Dv PRIV_NETINET_REUSEPORT
.El
.Ss Capability Plane
On 5BSD the clock time provider
.Xr BSDTime 8
claims the capability plane's clock gate
.Pq Dv SYS_GATE_SETTIME ,
which refuses the clock privileges to every process that does not hold the
gate, root included.
While
.Nm
is loaded and enabled it hands the configured uid to that gate.
A process running as that user (real and effective uid) passes it for
.Dv PRIV_ADJTIME ,
.Dv PRIV_CLOCK_SETTIME
and
.Dv PRIV_NTP_ADJTIME ,
and
.Xr ntpd 8
can step and slew the clock, only while it runs under the program-limited
grant
.Xr switchboard 8
gives the
ntpd
unit
.Pq Pa /Capabilities/Config/switchboard/service-authority.ucl
and executes
.Pa /usr/sbin/ntpd
or another image pinned in that grant.
A login shell holds no grant, so
.Ql su -m ntpd -c date ...
is refused, as is any program that is not pinned, even when it runs as the
ntpd user; the grant ends when the process executes another image.
An
.Xr ntpd 8
started with
.Ql service ntpd start
from a root shell has no grant and cannot discipline the clock; restart it
through
.Xr switchboard 8 .
The gate never accepts uid 0 this way, nor a process in capability mode, and
.Dv PRIV_SETTIMEOFDAY
stays refused.
Disabling or unloading
.Nm
withdraws it.
While the clock gate is claimed,
.Va security.mac.ntpd.uid
cannot be changed and
.Va security.mac.ntpd.enabled
can only be set to 0
.Pq Er EPERM ;
set them as
.Xr loader 8
tunables instead.
.Pp
.Pa /etc/rc.d/ntpd
warns at start when the capability plane is enabled and
.Xr ntpd 8
would be refused the clock: when it would run as root
.Po
.Va ntpd_user
set, files named in
.Va ntpd_flags
or
.Xr ntp.conf 5 ,
or this policy disabled
.Pc ,
or when
.Va ntpd_nice ,
.Va ntpd_fib ,
.Va ntpd_cpuset ,
.Va ntpd_chroot ,
.Va audit_user
or
.Va ntpd_prepend
make
.Xr rc.subr 8
execute a program that the grant does not pin before
.Xr ntpd 8 .
It fails the start when no
.Xr ntpd 8
is running a few seconds later.
.Pp
The grant pins programs, not their input:
root still controls
.Xr ntp.conf 5 ,
.Va ntpd_flags
and
.Va ntpd_program ,
and so which servers the granted
.Xr ntpd 8
follows and how it disciplines the clock.
This policy keeps other programs and root shells from setting the clock; it
does not contain root.
.Ss Runtime Configuration
The following
.Xr sysctl 8
MIBs are available for fine-tuning this MAC policy.
All
.Xr sysctl 8
variables can also be set as
.Xr loader 8
tunables in
.Xr loader.conf 5 .
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va security.mac.ntpd.enabled
Enable the
.Nm
policy.
(Default: 1).
.It Va security.mac.ntpd.uid
The numeric uid of the ntpd user.
(Default: 123).
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mac 4 ,
.Xr BSDTime 8 ,
.Xr ntpd 8
.Sh HISTORY
MAC first appeared in
.Fx 5.0
and
.Nm
first appeared in
.Fx 12.0 .
